---
source: crates/togi/tests/cli/mod.rs
expression: stdout(&ran)
---
Start or resume the night's tuning session in the foreground

Runs the night of the per-CCD tuning policy: controls, coverage mapping, qualification and reproduction, then cap tests and re-qualification while nothing else is due. The night has no deadline: it runs until you stop it (a signal, F10 in the dashboard, or a reboot), a conclusive failure at CO 0, no configured backend giving evidence any more, or a runtime safety dead end. A restart after a crash continues the night; a restart after a stop or a normal boot opens the next one. After a crash the next run attributes it from the journal and continues. On resume, known defects affecting past decisions are recorded with their cores; a too-aggressive one stops the run until reset --all starts a fresh session. It needs root.

A session starts only from a verified all-zero baseline and returns every core to CO 0 when it stops. A BIOS change found on resume is recorded and the session continues. A journal from an older ruleset, schema or evidence epoch, and no newer one, is archived. A newer ruleset, schema or evidence epoch stops the run before another event is written; reset --all archives that session. Journal lines are colored on terminals and in the system journal unless the environment asks for no colour.

Only one day observer, run or reset can own this machine, even with different state directories. Stop the day service before tuning. A busy /run/lock/togi.lock stops the command before any hardware access or event.

When stdin and stderr are terminals, run shows the session as the watch dashboard instead of one line per event, and prints the outcome when it stops: the restored offsets and why it stopped, or the dead end or error; events.jsonl still records every event. --no-tui prints the lines instead.

--tuning-boot runs the unattended service with an armed hardware watchdog. The first durable journal append resets its consecutive restart-limit count and records a pending leave reason once. Dead ends and incompatible journals persist a short leave reason before clearing the saved GRUB entry.

Usage: togi run [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <path>
          JSON configuration file [default: /etc/togi/config.json]

      --state-dir <path>
          State directory
          
          [default: /var/lib/togi]

      --tuning-boot <file>
          Run as the tuning boot service: persist a leave reason before restoring the day's boot selection, using a GRUB environment or /etc/togi/night.json, and reboot after a boot loop

      --no-tui
          Print one line per event instead of the dashboard on a terminal

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  sudo togi run            Tune this machine tonight until you stop it or a dead end does
  sudo togi run --no-tui   Print one line per event instead of the dashboard
